{
  "step_1": "The decision reason involves assessing the skewness of each lottery, defined as a greater portion of probability being assigned to unfavorable outcomes relative to favorable ones. This requires analyzing the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. For Lottery A: 100% probability on 2400 Euros (a single outcome, no variation). For Lottery B: 33% on 2500 (favorable), 66% on 2400 (slightly less favorable), and 1% on 0 (unfavorable). The skewness can be evaluated by comparing the concentration of probability on lower vs. higher outcomes. Lottery B has a small probability (1%) on a very low outcome (0), which could contribute to right-skewness if the bulk of probability is on lower outcomes. However, 66% is on 2400 (only slightly below 2500), and 33% on 2500, so the distribution is nearly symmetric with a small tail on the low end. Thus, Lottery B has a slight right skew due to the 1% chance of 0. Lottery A is not skewed (deterministic). Therefore, the reason can be applied: it prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which would be B.",
  "step_2": "The individual considered only the certainty of the outcome in Lottery A. The justification explicitly states: 'Option A, weil dort der Gewinn sicher ist' (Option A, because the win is certain there). The individual focused solely on the absence of risk in Lottery A and did not mention any consideration of probability distribution, tail events, or skewness. No reference was made to the 1% chance of 0 in Lottery B, nor to the relative probabilities of different outcomes. The deliberation process is based entirely on risk aversion due to certainty, not on the shape of the distribution or skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness—specifically, whether a lottery assigns a greater portion of probability to unfavorable outcomes. The individual did not consider skewness, the distribution of probabilities across outcomes, or the presence of low-probability extreme outcomes. Instead, the justification was based purely on the certainty of the outcome. The information considered (certainty) and the justification (safety of outcome) do not align with the skewness-based rule. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based reason to make the decision. The final assessment is 0, as the reason was clearly not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
